!! MCP Server — Detailed Instructions

This tiddler describes how to use each tool provided by the TiddlyWiki MCP server. For a general overview, see [[Basics]].

!! Setup

!!! Starting the Server

```
tiddlywiki ./mywiki --mcp
```

Replace `./mywiki` with the path to your wiki folder. The wiki folder must contain a `tiddlywiki.info` file. MCP write tools save their tiddler files themselves; browser edits reach disk only when `tiddlywiki/filesystem` is in your `tiddlywiki.info`.

!!! Browser access

To also access the wiki in a browser while the MCP server is running, add the `sse` keyword:

```
tiddlywiki ./mywiki --mcp sse port=8080
```

Without `port=`, or with `port=8080`, the server takes the first free port from 8080 upwards: a second wiki gets 8081, a third 8082. Any other `port=` must be free, or the server stops with an error. A port counts as used if any server answers on it at `127.0.0.1` or `[::1]`.

`sse` implies HTTP listen, plus pushes tiddler changes to the browser in real time via Server-Sent Events (~1s latency). Use `listen` instead for polling only: the plugin sets `$:/config/SyncPollingInterval` to 600000, so the browser refreshes every 10 minutes. See [[SSE Sync]] for transport details.

!!! Registering with an AI client

See [[Installation]] for `.mcp.json` setup (Claude Code) and [[Gemini Configuration]] for Gemini CLI.

!!! Read-Write Mode

The server starts in ''readonly mode by default''. To enable write operations, add `rw` (or `readwrite`) after `--mcp`:

```
tiddlywiki ./mywiki --mcp rw
```

In readonly mode (the default), only read tools are available: <$list filter="[tag[Read Tools]removeprefix[Instructions/]sort[]]" join=", "><code><$text text=<<currentTiddler>>/></code></$list>. `resave_tiddler` and `reload_mcp_modules` are write tools and require `rw`.

!!! Label

Give your MCP server instance a name to identify it in the `.tw-mcp/connect` discovery file. Useful when running multiple instances for different wikis or from different tools (e.g. VS Code, Claude CLI, terminal):

```
tiddlywiki ./mywiki --mcp label=vscode
```

The label appears in the `.tw-mcp/connect` JSON and helps distinguish which process created the pipe. Started from VS Code or Claude Code, the server appends the workspace and wiki folder names (e.g. `vscode:myproject/mywiki`); without `label=` that tag alone becomes the label.

!!! Path Restrictions

By default, file operations are restricted to the wiki's `tiddlers/`, `files/`, and `output/` subdirectories. You can override this with:

```
tiddlywiki ./mywiki --mcp allowed-paths=/path/one,/path/two
```

!!! Several Wikis From One Client (Route Mode)

If you work on several wikis, each in its own subfolder of one folder, a single MCP entry can reach all of them:

```
tiddlywiki ./any-wiki --mcp route=/path/to/editions
```

* Start and stop each wiki server as usual. The router finds the running ones through their `.tw-mcp/connect` files, so the AI client never needs to be restarted
* Every tool takes an extra `wiki` argument: the folder name, the label or the HTTP port. Without it, a call goes to the wiki on port 8080. Change this with `default-port=<n>`
* [[list_wikis|Instructions/list_wikis]] shows which wikis are running right now
* Client setup: see [[Installation]], section "Several wikis: one entry for all"
* Like a proxy, the router is readonly by default. Add `rw` to forward write tools
* `./any-wiki` only hosts the tw-mcp plugins. Route mode serves no wiki of its own

---

!! Transports

The server runs two transports simultaneously:

!!! stdio

Standard input/output — the primary transport used by Claude CLI and other MCP clients. Messages are newline-delimited JSON-RPC 2.0.

!!! Named Pipe

A local named pipe for helper tools (like `tw-upload`). On startup, the server:

# Generates a 256-bit random authentication token
# Creates a named pipe (`\\.\pipe\tiddlywiki-mcp-<wiki-id>` on Windows, `.mcp.sock` on Unix)
# Writes a `.tw-mcp/connect` discovery file to the wiki directory (to the first `includeWikis` entry when there is one, so editions sharing a wiki share one server):

```json
{"pipe":"\\\\.\\pipe\\tiddlywiki-mcp-...","token":"<hex>","pid":12345,"label":"vscode","listen":true,"port":8080}
```

Pipe clients must send the token as `wikilabs.tw-mcp/auth` inside the `_meta` of every request, not once at connection time. The `pid` field lets clients verify the server process is still alive. The `label` field (if present) identifies which instance created the pipe. The `listen` field is `true` when this process serves HTTP, and `port` gives its port (absent otherwise).

On exit, the server cleans up the pipe and discovery file.

